{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3277","artifact_type":"architect","name":"plan/architect-bookend-validator-wiring.md@sha256:e18444c345c6c3a1fb89cb6bd903ba631e9c5fa9bd1fef0ddda8d8a19d712eb3"},"owner_reference":"3277","content":"# Architecture Spec: Bookend Validator Wiring (#3277)\n\n**Scope**: WHAT to build \u2014 call sites, control flow, exception contract, data model, test scope. No implementation code. `BookendValidator` (`sam_schema/core/dependencies.py:276-393`) is not modified.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\n\n`BookendValidator` is correct and fully unit-tested but has zero callers in non-test code. The fix wires it into the two `dh_core/operations.py` write paths that can put a plan into `state=ready`: `create_plan` (line 202) and `finalize_plan` (line 668). `append_task` (line 621) is deliberately **not** gated. A new `BookendValidationError` exception (in `sam_schema/core/exceptions.py`, alongside `PlanNotFoundError`/`TaskValidationError`) is raised synchronously in the write path, before the backend call, so nothing is ever persisted from a rejected write. It propagates uncaught through `dh_core.operations` exactly like `PlanNotFoundError` does today: FastMCP auto-converts it to `ToolError` at the MCP transport, and two `sam_plan.py` Typer commands need one line added to their existing `except` tuples to keep the CLI's clean-exit contract.\n\nThe governing principle, derived from re-reading all three backends' `create_plan`/`finalize_plan` implementations (\u00a73): **validate exactly at the moment a plan-write operation would leave the plan in `state=ready`, and at no other moment.** This single rule produces both gated call sites, explains why `append_task` is excluded, and answers the backward-compatibility question without any extra migration logic.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Root Cause Recap (why two call sites, not one)\n\nThe initial hypothesis \u2014 \"gate only `finalize_plan`, since that's the drafting\u2192ready transition\" \u2014 is wrong, and re-reading the three backends' `create_plan` shows why:\n\n| Backend | `create_plan` state assignment |\n|---|---|\n| `local_yaml.py:373` | `state = DRAFTING` only when `not tasks`; otherwise the `Plan(...)` constructor's default (`PlanState.READY`, `models.py:288`) applies unmodified |\n| `github_task.py:376,488` | `plan_data[\"state\"] = PlanState.DRAFTING` only when the drafting marker is written, which only happens for the empty-tasks path |\n| `beads.py:610` | `\"state\": PlanState.DRAFTING if not tasks else PlanState.READY` (explicit, same rule) |\n\nAll three backends implement the same rule: **`create_plan(tasks=[...])` with a non-empty task list produces an immediately-`ready` plan.** No `finalize_plan` call ever happens for that path \u2014 `swarm-task-planner.md:199`'s one-shot pattern (`create` with the full `T1..T6` list in a single call) is exactly this path, and it is almost certainly how `Pb31107bf` was registered. Gating only `finalize_plan` would leave this exact reproduction case (AC #4) uncaught. `swarm-task-planner.md:211-227`'s incremental pattern (`create` with `tasks=[]`, repeated `append_task`, then `finalize`) is the only path that reaches `state=ready` via `finalize_plan`.\n\n`append_task` (all three backends) never assigns `state` \u2014 it only appends to the task list. It cannot be the moment a plan becomes ready, so under the governing principle it is never a validation gate.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Component Design\n\n### 3.1 `sam_schema/core/exceptions.py` \u2014 new exception\n\nAdd one class, following the existing `TaskValidationError`/`PlanNotFoundError` shape exactly (message pre-formatted in `__init__`, public attributes for programmatic access, subclass of `SamError`). Add `\"BookendValidationError\"` to `__all__`.\n\n```python\nclass BookendValidationError(SamError):\n    \"\"\"Raised when a plan-write operation would leave bookend tasks structurally invalid.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, plan_id: str, errors: list[str]) -> None: ...\n    # attributes: self.plan_id: str, self.errors: list[str]\n    # message: f\"Plan {plan_id} failed bookend validation: {'; '.join(errors)}\"\n```\n\n`errors` is the verbatim return value of `BookendValidator.validate()` \u2014 no reformatting, so the human-readable strings the validator already produces (e.g. `\"Plan has acceptance-criteria-structured but no T0 baseline task...\"`) reach the caller unchanged.\n\n### 3.2 `dh_core/operations.py` \u2014 imports\n\n- Line 73: extend `from sam_schema.core.dependencies import DependencyGraph` to also import `BookendValidator`.\n- Line 74: extend `from sam_schema.core.exceptions import ConcurrentClaimUnsupportedError, PlanNotFoundError, SamError, TaskNotFoundError` to also import `BookendValidationError`.\n- `PlanState` and `Plan` are already imported (lines 85-87) \u2014 no new import needed for either.\n\n### 3.3 `create_plan` (operations.py:202-269) \u2014 control flow insertion\n\nInsertion point: **after** `normalized_tasks` is fully built (after line 252), **before** `backend.create_plan(...)` is called (line 254).\n\nLogic:\n\n1. If `normalized_tasks` is empty (`not normalized_tasks`) \u2014 the incremental-start call \u2014 skip validation entirely and fall through to the existing `backend.create_plan(...)` call unchanged. This is not an optimization; it is required correctness: a caller starting the incremental pattern may legitimately declare `acceptance_criteria_structured` at creation time with zero tasks, and running `BookendValidator` against an empty task list would falsely reject that call under Rule 5, closing off the only conforming way to build a plan incrementally.\n2. If `normalized_tasks` is non-empty, construct a transient, unpersisted `Plan` for validation purposes only:\n   `Plan(feature=slug, goal=goal, tasks=normalized_tasks, acceptance_criteria_structured=list(acceptance_criteria_structured or []))` \u2014 mirrors `local_yaml.py:358-368`'s own construction (`feature=slug`), and supplies only the two fields `BookendValidator.validate()` actually reads (`self._plan.tasks`, `self._plan.acceptance_criteria_structured`; see `dependencies.py:350,373,383`). No `plan_id` exists yet at this point \u2014 none is needed for validation.\n3. Call `BookendValidator(transient_plan).validate()`. If the returned list is non-empty, raise `BookendValidationError(plan_id=slug, errors=errors)` immediately \u2014 `backend.create_plan(...)` is never called, so nothing is written to any backend (local YAML file, GitHub issue, or beads) on rejection.\n4. If the list is empty, proceed exactly as today.\n\nThis also transparently handles plans with tasks but no structured criteria (a plan with only `T1` and no `acceptance_criteria_structured`): `BookendValidator.validate()` already no-ops in that case per its own docstring (`dependencies.py:280-281`, \"Plans without bookend tasks and without structured criteria always pass validation\") \u2014 no extra conditional is needed to preserve AC #3.\n\n### 3.4 `finalize_plan` (operations.py:668-699) \u2014 control flow insertion\n\nInsertion point: **before** `backend.finalize_plan(plan)` is called (line 698).\n\nLogic:\n\n1. Read the plan as it currently stands: reuse the existing `read_plan(backend, plan)` operation (same module, line 272) rather than duplicating `Plan.model_validate(...)` \u2014 it already does exactly this and already raises `PlanNotFoundError` when `plan` doesn't resolve, which is the correct, pre-existing failure mode to keep (do not change it).\n2. If `current.plan.state == PlanState.READY` already, **skip validation** and fall through to `backend.finalize_plan(plan)` unchanged. This is the backward-compatibility mechanism (\u00a74): it mirrors `local_yaml.py:737-738`'s own idempotent short-circuit (`if plan.state == PlanState.READY: return {...}` without re-validating), so a repeat `finalize` call on an already-ready plan \u2014 including a plan that was grandfathered in non-compliant before this fix shipped \u2014 keeps succeeding as a no-op. Validating unconditionally on every call would instead turn every subsequent idempotent `finalize` on a pre-existing non-compliant `ready` plan into a new failure, which is not this fix's job.\n3. If `current.plan.state != PlanState.READY` (the plan is actually about to transition), call `BookendValidator(current.plan).validate()`. If non-empty, raise `BookendValidationError(plan_id=current.plan.plan_id or plan, errors=errors)` before `backend.finalize_plan(plan)` runs \u2014 the plan stays in `drafting`, unchanged, and the caller can `append_task` the missing `T0`/`TN` and retry `finalize` (the repair path stays open; `append_task` is never gated, so it can always be used to fix a plan that failed at `finalize`).\n4. Otherwise proceed exactly as today.\n\n### 3.5 `append_task` (operations.py:621-665) \u2014 no change\n\nNot modified. Per \u00a72, it never assigns `state`, so under the governing principle it is never a gate. This is also the repair path referenced in \u00a73.4 step 3.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Backward-Compatibility Decision (resolves feature-context Open Questions #1-#3)\n\n**Decision: hard error, no warn-first period, no auto-correction. Enforcement applies to `create_plan` (non-empty-tasks path) and `finalize_plan` (actual drafting\u2192ready transitions) only; already-`ready` plans are never re-validated.**\n\nRationale:\n\n- The \"gate the ready-transition, not the plan\" rule in \u00a73.4 step 2 **is** the backward-compatibility answer, not a separate mechanism bolted on afterward: any plan already sitting in `state=ready` \u2014 compliant or not, created before or after this fix ships \u2014 is structurally exempt from every future call, because none of those calls re-validates an already-`ready` plan. No plan enumeration, no data migration, no backfill script is needed to ship this safely (resolves Open Question #5's stated prerequisite \u2014 it was a prerequisite for a *warn-first* rollout, which this design does not do).\n- **`Pb31107bf` (Open Question #2)**: if it is already `state=ready` (the likely case, since its `T1-T6` implementation tasks are already mid-dispatch per the item's own observation \u2014 dispatch requires `ready`, see `_sam_plan_ready`'s drafting-check), it is untouched by this change. Item #3268 ships without an empirical TN verification pass for that plan, which is an accepted, bounded consequence \u2014 not a defect this fix introduces. If it is still `drafting` (unlikely but possible), the next `finalize_plan` call against it will now correctly fail, and the repair path (`append_task` a `T0` and `TN`, then retry `finalize`) is available. Either way, no special-case code for this specific plan is needed.\n- A warn-then-block rollout was rejected: it requires exactly the plan-population enumeration Open Question #5 flags as not yet done, needs a second future change to close the gap, and the \"gate the transition\" design already achieves the compatibility goal without it.\n- Auto-correction (synthesizing missing bookend tasks) was rejected: `create_plan`/`finalize_plan` would silently mutate the caller's task list, which is a bigger surprise than a rejected write, and it would require the write path to know how to construct a `t0-baseline-capture`/`tn-verification-gate` task correctly (agent name, dependency wiring) \u2014 logic that belongs to `swarm-task-planner`, not to the validation gate. Out of scope here.\n- Open Question #4 (`complete-implementation`'s Pre-Phase-1 conflation of \"artifact absent\" with \"no structured criteria,\" `SKILL.md:300-324,318`) is **out of scope for this spec** \u2014 it is a downstream consequence that becomes less likely to matter once enforcement is real, but fixing that gate's own logic is a separate change with its own acceptance criteria, per the feature-context's own framing (\"a consequence of the fix, not necessarily a change made by it\").\n\nThis reasoning is also consistent with the generalist industry framing surfaced by research for this spec: schema-level (Pydantic field) validation is a necessary syntactic layer but is not itself a correctness guarantee for cross-field business invariants (\"bookend presence/wiring given structured criteria\" spans multiple fields and multiple tasks) \u2014 that class of invariant belongs at a semantic/service-layer inspection step ahead of persistence, which is exactly where `BookendValidator` already lived, unwired. (General pattern, not project-specific: see search results on multi-layer validation \u2014 syntactic parse, semantic/business-rule inspection, then store.)\n\n---\n\n## 5. Error Propagation Contract\n\n`BookendValidationError(SamError)` propagates exactly like `PlanNotFoundError` does today \u2014 no new propagation mechanism, reusing the existing, already-proven boundary behavior:\n\n| Layer | Behavior | Change needed |\n|---|---|---|\n| `dh_core/operations.py` | Raised uncaught from `create_plan`/`finalize_plan` | New `raise` statements (\u00a73.3, \u00a73.4) |\n| `sam_schema/server.py` (`_sam_plan_create`, `_sam_plan_finalize`) | Thin adapters; do not currently catch `PlanNotFoundError` either \u2014 exceptions propagate uncaught out of the `@mcp.tool` function | **None.** Confirmed by `tests_sam/test_server.py:139-160` etc.: direct in-process calls raise the raw `SamError` subclass; FastMCP's transport layer converts any uncaught exception from inside a tool call to `ToolError` (`isError=true`) automatically when invoked through the actual MCP transport. `BookendValidationError` gets this for free. |\n| `sam_schema/sam_plan.py` `create` (line 262) | Catches `(ValidationError, ValueError, OSError)` around the `operations.create_plan` call (line 292) and calls `_error(str(exc))` (exit code 1) | **Add `BookendValidationError` to this tuple.** Without it, the CLI exits via an unhandled traceback instead of the clean `_error()` path every other validation failure on this command uses. |\n| `sam_schema/sam_plan.py` `finalize` (line 529) | Catches `(PlanNotFoundError, FileNotFoundError, FormatDetectionError)` around `operations.finalize_plan` (line 540) and calls `_error(str(exc), 2 if FormatDetectionError else 1)` | **Add `BookendValidationError` to this tuple**, exit code 1 (same bucket as `PlanNotFoundError`). |\n| `sam_schema/sam_plan.py` `append-task` (line 483) | Not touched \u2014 `append_task` is not gated | None |\n\n`CreatePlanError` (the existing `BaseModel` return-value error shape checked via `isinstance` in both `server.py:242` and `sam_plan.py:294`) is **not** the mechanism used here \u2014 `BookendValidationError` is a raised exception, matching `PlanNotFoundError`/`TaskValidationError`'s existing convention for this exact function, not `CreatePlanError`'s convention (which is documented as the `ArtifactWriteError` path). Do not conflate the two error-reporting styles that already coexist on `create_plan`; see `<concerns>` below.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Data Model\n\nNo schema changes. `BookendValidationError` (\u00a73.1) is the only new type. The transient `Plan` constructed in `create_plan` (\u00a73.3 step 2) is not a new type \u2014 it is a plain, unpersisted instance of the existing `Plan` model, discarded immediately after `.validate()` returns (or replaced by the real persisted plan on the success path, which `backend.create_plan(...)` constructs independently and which is not the object we validated \u2014 they must be constructed with the same `tasks`/`acceptance_criteria_structured` inputs, which they are, since both read from the same `normalized_tasks`/`acceptance_criteria_structured` locals).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Testing Architecture\n\n### 7.1 Non-regression (AC #6)\n\n`tests_sam/test_dependencies.py` is not modified. Nothing in this design changes `BookendValidator`, its constructor signature, or `validate()`'s return contract.\n\n### 7.2 New integration test (AC #5) \u2014 through `operations.py`, not the validator directly\n\nLocation: append a new test class to `tests/test_operations_sam.py` (existing file already exercises `dh_core`/`backlog_core` operations against `ContentTaskProvider(InMemoryBackend())` \u2014 no filesystem, no GitHub, no beads; reuse this fixture rather than introducing a new one). Import `create_plan`, `finalize_plan`, `append_task` from `dh_core.operations`, `BookendValidationError` from `sam_schema.core.exceptions`, and `AcceptanceCriterion`, `BookendType` from `sam_schema.core.models` alongside the file's existing `Task`/`TaskStatus` import.\n\nRequired cases (all call `dh_core.operations` functions directly against a `TaskBackend`, never `BookendValidator` directly):\n\n1. **`create_plan` rejects (AC #1, #4)**: `create_plan(backend, slug=..., goal=..., tasks=[<one non-bookend Task>], acceptance_criteria_structured=[<one AcceptanceCriterion>])` raises `BookendValidationError`. This is the direct reproduction of `Pb31107bf`'s one-shot registration shape.\n2. **`create_plan` accepts unchanged (AC #2)**: same call, but `tasks` includes a correctly-wired `T0` (`bookend_type=BookendType.T0_BASELINE`, `dependencies=[]`), one implementation task, and a correctly-wired `TN` (`bookend_type=BookendType.TN_VERIFICATION`, `dependencies=[<implementation task id>]`) \u2014 succeeds, returns `CreatePlanResult` as today.\n3. **`create_plan` with empty criteria is unaffected (AC #3)**: `tasks=[<one non-bookend Task>]`, no `acceptance_criteria_structured` \u2014 succeeds unchanged, no bookends required.\n4. **`finalize_plan` rejects the incremental-build path (AC #1, #4, #5's \"through operations.py\" requirement)**: `create_plan(backend, ..., tasks=[], acceptance_criteria_structured=[<one criterion>])` (drafting), `append_task(backend, plan_id, <one non-bookend Task>)` (still missing bookends \u2014 demonstrates `append_task` is intentionally *not* rejected here even though the plan is non-compliant), then `finalize_plan(backend, plan_id)` raises `BookendValidationError`.\n5. **`finalize_plan` grandfathers an already-`ready` plan (\u00a74 backward-compatibility guarantee)**: construct a plan directly `ready` with non-compliant bookends (via `create_plan` with a non-empty, non-compliant task list \u2014 which itself would now raise per case 1, so this case must instead write the non-compliant `ready` plan directly through the backend/provider below the `dh_core.operations` layer, simulating a plan that existed before this fix shipped), then call `finalize_plan` on it and assert it succeeds as a no-op instead of raising \u2014 proves the exemption in \u00a73.4 step 2 is real, not just asserted in prose.\n\nEach case fails before the fix and passes after, per AC #5's explicit requirement.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Out of Scope (reaffirmed from feature-context)\n\n- `BookendValidator`'s own rules/logic \u2014 untouched.\n- The three backends' `create_plan`/`finalize_plan`/`append_task` bodies \u2014 untouched; the gate sits entirely in `dh_core/operations.py`, above all three, per the feature-context's single-source-of-truth constraint.\n- `complete-implementation/SKILL.md`'s Pre-Phase-1 conflation bug (Open Question #4) \u2014 separate follow-up.\n- Backfilling `Pb31107bf` or any other existing plan \u2014 no plan content is modified by this change.\n- `swarm-task-planner.md`'s Phase 4 Step 12 prose self-check \u2014 left as-is; it now duplicates a real gate rather than being the only check, which is an acceptable, documented overlap (feature-context Risk #5), not something this spec resolves.\n\n---\n\n<concerns>\n1. **Pre-existing return-type/exception-style contradiction on `create_plan` that this design must not imitate.** `dh_core/operations.py::create_plan`'s signature is annotated `-> CreatePlanResult` (operations.py:212) and its body (202-269, read in full) only ever constructs and returns `CreatePlanResult` \u2014 there is no code path that returns `CreatePlanError`. Yet `sam_schema/server.py:242` and `sam_schema/sam_plan.py:294` both do `isinstance(result, CreatePlanError)` on that same return value, and `CreatePlanError`'s own docstring (`models.py:680-685`) says it's \"Returned by `operations.create_plan` on the `ArtifactWriteError` path (ADR-2509-5).\" I could not find that path in the current `create_plan` body \u2014 `ArtifactWriteError` is only mentioned in `create_plan`'s own `Raises:` docstring (operations.py:235), suggesting it's actually *raised*, not caught-and-converted. Either the `isinstance(result, CreatePlanError)` checks in both callers are dead code left over from a prior implementation, or `create_plan` lost a `try/except ArtifactWriteError` block at some point without its callers being updated. This is unverified beyond what I could read in this pass \u2014 worth a follow-up fact-check \u2014 but it means: (a) `create_plan`'s actual current error-reporting story is `CreatePlanResult` (success) or a raised exception (failure), full stop, which is exactly the `BookendValidationError` model this spec uses, and (b) the codebase already has one instance of a documented-but-seemingly-unreachable \"structured error object\" convention living side by side with the exception convention on the very function this spec modifies. Flagging so `BookendValidationError` isn't mistaken for a `CreatePlanError`-style return value during implementation, and so someone eventually resolves whether the `isinstance` checks are dead.\n2. **`append-task` CLI command silently swallows a real, already-raised exception type**, unrelated to this fix but discovered while establishing the CLI error-propagation precedent this spec relies on (\u00a75). `TaskValidationError` is genuinely raised by every backend's `append_task`/`create_plan` for duplicate task IDs (confirmed: `local_yaml.py:346-347`, `github_task.py:300,450`, `beads.py:506-508,854`, `_utils.py:39`), and `dh_core.operations.append_task`'s own docstring (operations.py:653-654) documents it as a raised error for that function. But `sam_plan.py`'s `append-task` Typer command (line 524) only catches `(ValidationError, ValueError, PlanNotFoundError, FileNotFoundError, FormatDetectionError)` \u2014 `TaskValidationError` is absent from that tuple. A duplicate-task-ID append via the CLI today produces an unhandled Python traceback instead of the clean `_error()` exit every sibling command in this file uses. This is a pre-existing gap on a code path adjacent to, but not touched by, this spec (append_task is intentionally left ungated here) \u2014 I did not fix it since it's outside #3277's acceptance criteria, but it's the same class of bug this spec is careful not to introduce for `BookendValidationError` on `create`/`finalize` (\u00a75), and would be a reasonable one-line follow-up.\n</concerns>\n\n---\n\n## Post-Implementation Annotation (QG Context Refinement, P2b19c9aa/T6, 2026-08-29)\n\nImplementation matches this spec exactly. Verified against `S6-code-review-T1-bookend-validator-wiring` (verdict PASS, T1-T4 all PASS) and `TN-verification-bookend-validator-wiring` (verdict PASS, all 6 structured acceptance criteria fail-to-pass or pass-to-pass as designed, 3926 passed / 42 skipped / 5 xfailed on the full regression suite). No divergence notes were recorded on any of Pf1e7e555's tasks (T0-T5, all `divergence-notes: 0`).\n\n**Concerns block status \u2014 still open, not filed as follow-ups.** Both items in the `<concerns>` block above (the `CreatePlanError`/`isinstance` dead-code question on `create_plan`, and `append-task`'s missing `TaskValidationError` in its except-tuple) remain exactly as flagged pre-implementation: neither was fixed by this plan (correctly, per its own scope), and `backlog_list_followups(followup_to=\"3277\")` returns zero linked items, so neither has been captured as a trackable follow-up. Recommend filing both as separate backlog items if they are to be acted on rather than re-discovered later.\n\n**\u00a78 Out of Scope item re-examined by an independent audit.** The QG doc-drift-auditor (dispatched separately against this item, `doc-drift-audit-bookend-validator-wiring`) re-derived a root-cause hypothesis for the original defect \u2014 \"the swarm-task-planner agent itself may not be using the generation logic\" \u2014 that is in tension with this item's own Root-Cause Analysis (a code/schema gap: the validator existed but had zero callers). Both are compatible, not contradictory: this spec's fix closes the code/schema gap (a non-compliant plan-write is now rejected outright), which is sufficient regardless of whether `swarm-task-planner`'s own generation logic is independently correct \u2014 a planner that fails to generate bookends now produces a loud, actionable `BookendValidationError` instead of a silent pass-through. Whether the planner's generation logic itself is correct remains unverified and is not answered by this spec (see \u00a78's `swarm-task-planner.md` bullet and feature-context Open Question #4). No follow-up item exists for this either.\n"}